      { "value": "22", "label": "players: enough to spot a moderate difference, not a small one", "page": 3 }
    ],
    "chart": {
      "title": "Knee alignment angles",
      "a_label": "Painful knee",
      "b_label": "Pain-free knee",
      "unit": "°",
      "rows": [
        { "label": "aLDFA, distal femur", "a": [83.89, 1.78], "b": [83.17, 2.39] },
        { "label": "mLDFA, distal femur", "a": [88.59, 2.52], "b": [88.48, 2.24] },
        { "label": "aTFA, thigh to shin", "a": [176.25, 2.73], "b": [175.64, 2.49] },
        { "label": "aMPTA, upper tibia", "a": [88.00, 2.62], "b": [87.50, 2.93] },
        { "label": "mMPTA, upper tibia", "a": [87.03, 2.24], "b": [87.06, 2.16] },
        { "label": "aLDTA, lower tibia", "a": [87.73, 2.33], "b": [86.89, 2.49] },
        { "label": "mLDTA, lower tibia", "a": [87.92, 1.59], "b": [87.18, 1.88] }
      ],
      "source": "Paired t-tests, all p ≥ 0.107. Table 3, p. 6."
    },
    "table": {
      "columns": ["Angle (degrees)", "Painful knee", "Pain-free knee", "p"],
      "rows": [
        ["aLDFA, distal femur", "83.89 ± 1.78", "83.17 ± 2.39", "0.277"],
        ["mLDFA, distal femur", "88.59 ± 2.52", "88.48 ± 2.24", "0.883"],
        ["aTFA, thigh to shin", "176.25 ± 2.73", "175.64 ± 2.49", "0.252"],
        ["aMPTA, upper tibia", "88.00 ± 2.62", "87.50 ± 2.93", "0.456"],
        ["mMPTA, upper tibia", "87.03 ± 2.24", "87.06 ± 2.16", "0.948"],
        ["aLDTA, lower tibia", "87.73 ± 2.33", "86.89 ± 2.49", "0.130"],
        ["mLDTA, lower tibia", "87.92 ± 1.59", "87.18 ± 1.88", "0.107"]
      ],
      "source": "Mean ± SD, paired t-tests. Table 3, p. 6."
    },
    "bullets": [
      "None of the 7 reached significance. p-values ran from 0.107 to 0.948 (p. 6).",
      "The authors admit it: with 22 players, a small real difference could slip through. This doesn't rule a link out (p. 8).",
      "Their bet is on jump load and landing mechanics, which this study didn't measure (p. 7)."
    ]
  },

  "meaning": {
    "summary": "Both knees lined up almost the same. Look at how athletes jump and land before blaming their build.",
    "roles": [
      { "role": "Athletic Medicine", "line": "An X-ray likely won't explain the sore knee. Ask about jump volume." },
      { "role": "Athletic Performance", "line": "We can't train bone angles. We can coach landings." },
      { "role": "Applied Performance", "line": "Jump counts and landing data likely beat a static screen." },
      { "role": "Sport Coaches", "line": "Leg shape isn't a red flag. Watch jump volume in heavy weeks." }
    ]
  },

  "takeaways": {
    "bullets": [
      "Check jump load and landing before blaming alignment.",
      "Count jumps in practice and match them to knee pain.",
      "One static screen says little about tendon risk."
    ],
    "question": "When a knee tendon flares up, what do we check first? And is it something we can actually change?",
    "bottom_line": "Load and landing first. Leg shape second."
  },

  "readout": {
    "context": {
      "title": "Should we add a static alignment screen to catch jumper's knee before it starts?",
      "bullets": [
        "The question came up in screening: can we spot a knee that will hurt by how the leg is built?",
        "One study, 22 elite male volleyball players, each with pain in one knee and not the other.",
        "Both legs X-rayed standing, hip to ankle. 7 alignment angles, read by a blinded examiner.",
        "Constraint: standing X-rays only. No jump counts, no landing data, one team, one country."
      ]
    },
    "finding": {
      "title": "Both knees line up the same: 0 of 7 angles differed",
      "bullets": [
        "None of the 7 angles differed between the painful and the pain free knee. p ran 0.107 to 0.948 (p. 6).",
        "The biggest side to side gap was under 1 degree, read off a standing X-ray (p. 6).",
        "22 players can catch a moderate difference, not a small one, so a small link is not ruled out (p. 8)."
      ]
    },
    "recommendation": {
      "title": "Skip the alignment screen. Spend the time on jump load.",
      "action": "Do not add static alignment screening to the jumper's knee workup. Put that staff time into counting jumps and watching landing quality in volleyball and basketball.",
      "confidence": "Preliminary. 22 players, one team, one plane of movement, no dynamic data. A small real alignment effect could still be there, so this is a call on where screening time goes, not proof.",
      "validation": "Match weekly jump counts against reported knee pain this season. Reassess in January when we review the screening battery, and revisit if a dynamic or longitudinal study lands."
    }
  }
}

